---
import EnglishBreadcrumb from '../../../../components/english/EnglishBreadcrumb.astro'
import { ContractChangeLab } from '../../../../components/english/ContractChangeLab'
import EnglishArticleLayout from '../../../../layouts/EnglishArticleLayout.astro'
import {
  BASE_CONTRACT_FIELDS,
  buildContractReference,
} from '../../../../features/english-contracts/model'
import contractStylesUrl from '../../../../styles/layouts/english-data-contracts.css?url'
import { getEnglishRoute } from '../../../../utils/english-routes'

const title = 'Data Contracts: Producer & Consumer Interfaces | sql.sb'
const description =
  'What a data contract actually contains — fields, types, nullability, semantics and keys — and which producer changes break consumers loudly or silently.'

const reference = buildContractReference()
const fields = BASE_CONTRACT_FIELDS
---

<EnglishArticleLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  active="warehouse"
  styleUrls={[contractStylesUrl]}
>
  <EnglishBreadcrumb
    items={[
      { label: 'English home', key: 'overview' },
      { label: 'Data warehouse', key: 'warehouse' },
      { label: 'Data contracts' },
    ]}
  />

  <header class="english-article__header">
    <p class="english-article__eyebrow">Data warehouse · governance</p>
    <h1 class="english-article__title">
      Data contracts: what producers and consumers actually agree on
    </h1>
    <p class="english-article__lede">
      A pipeline fails loudly when a column disappears. It often keeps running — with every number
      100× too large — when a column quietly changes meaning. A data contract is how the second case
      becomes a decision instead of an incident.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="english-answer">
    <span class="english-answer__label">Quick answer</span>
    <p>
      A data contract is an explicit, versioned agreement between the producer of a dataset and its
      consumers: which fields exist, their types, nullability and meaning, which keys identify a
      row, and how any of that may change. The contract is enforced by tests that run with the
      producer's change and the consumer's build, so a breaking change fails in review instead of in
      production. It answers a different question from a quality check: the check validates the
      batch that was produced; the contract defines what the producer promised.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="english-article__body">
    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="interface">
      <h2 id="interface">The dataset is an interface, not just a table</h2>
      <p>
        Consider the account balance snapshot that feeds the daily report, the branch dashboard and
        the regulatory extract. Three consumers read the same table, but each one depends on a
        different part of its meaning. The contract has to state all of it explicitly.
      </p>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table">
          <caption>Contract fields for the account balance snapshot</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Field</th>
              <th scope="col">Type</th>
              <th scope="col">Meaning</th>
              <th scope="col">Consumed by</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {fields.map((field) => (
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <code>{field.name}</code>
                  {field.nullable ? '' : ' · not null'}
                </td>
                <td>
                  <code>{field.type}</code>
                </td>
                <td>{field.semantics}</td>
                <td>{field.consumedBy.join(', ')}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The type alone is not the contract. <code>balance</code> is <code>decimal(18,2)</code> in
        both versions of a unit change — the field is the same, the meaning is not, and no schema
        diff will show it.
      </p>
      <ContractChangeLab client:load />
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="classes">
      <h2 id="classes">Eight changes, five ways to get hurt</h2>
      <p>
        Producer changes fall into classes with very different failure modes. The classification
        that matters is not “does the schema change?” but “how does the change reach a consumer?”
      </p>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table">
          <caption>Producer changes and how consumers experience them</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Change</th>
              <th scope="col">Field</th>
              <th scope="col">Compatibility</th>
              <th scope="col">Detected</th>
              <th scope="col">Consumer effect</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {reference.map((row) => (
              <tr data-tone={row.consumerEffect === 'silent-wrong' ? 'wrong' : undefined}>
                <td>{row.label}</td>
                <td>
                  <code>{row.field}</code>
                </td>
                <td>{row.compatibility}</td>
                <td>{row.detectedAt}</td>
                <td>{row.consumerEffect}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Additive.</strong> A new nullable column is compatible, but it still owes the
          consumer a meaning and a backfill plan.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Type change.</strong> Narrowing <code>decimal(18,2)</code> to{' '}
          <code>decimal(12,2)</code> passes on small data and overflows on one large balance.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Semantic change.</strong> Yuan to cents inside the same column is a breaking
          change with an unchanged schema.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Structural change.</strong> Renames and removals fail loudly, which is why they
          need a deprecation window rather than an emergency hotfix.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Value-domain change.</strong> A new currency code keeps queries running but can
          leave a consumer with no rate, label or rule for the new member.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="silent">
      <h2 id="silent">Why silent risks are the expensive ones</h2>
      <p>
        Loud failures cost time: a job stops, an alert fires, someone restarts the pipeline. Silent
        failures cost trust: the dashboard keeps rendering, the total is wrong by a factor of 100,
        and the error is discovered weeks later by a business user.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Unit change.</strong> The same column name stores a different unit; every sum,
          average and threshold built on it is wrong.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Nullability relaxation.</strong> <code>branch_id</code> becomes nullable; the
          branch join drops the unknown rows with no error, so branch totals quietly shrink.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Enum addition.</strong> A new currency code arrives; a consumer with a closed
          mapping labels it as “other” and the data looks present but is unusable.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="english-callout" data-tone="warning">
        <strong>A schema test cannot see a meaning change</strong>
        <p>
          Contracts therefore have to state semantics and units in words, and reviewers have to
          treat a semantics-only change as a versioned change.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="process">
      <h2 id="process">The change process is part of the contract</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>The producer declares the change</strong> in the contract file: field, type,
          nullable, meaning and version.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The producer's CI runs the contract test</strong> against every registered
          consumer expectation. A breaking change fails before merge.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Consumers get a deprecation window</strong> for renames and removals: the old name
          stays, the new name is dual-written, and every listed consumer signs off.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Semantic changes are versioned</strong> rather than made in place: add{' '}
          <code>balance_cents</code>, dual-write it, migrate consumers, then retire the old field.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ownership is explicit</strong> for the field and for every consumer, so “who
          updates the dashboard query?” has an answer before the change ships.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        A contract test is small: given the current contract, assert that the columns the consumer
        selects exist with compatible types and nullability, and that the meanings it relies on are
        still documented. It does not need a schema registry to be useful at this scale.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="boundaries">
      <h2 id="boundaries">Contract, quality check, observability</h2>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table">
          <caption>Three practices, three questions</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Practice</th>
              <th scope="col">Question</th>
              <th scope="col">Typical failure it prevents</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr data-tone="right">
              <td>
                <strong>Data contract</strong>
              </td>
              <td>What did the producer agree to provide, and how may it change?</td>
              <td>A release that removes or repurposes a field consumers rely on</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                <strong>Quality check</strong>
              </td>
              <td>Is this produced batch usable?</td>
              <td>A batch that satisfies the contract but contains broken rows</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>
                <strong>Observability</strong>
              </td>
              <td>Is the pipeline healthy over time?</td>
              <td>A slow drift that no single batch reveals</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The three are complementary. A contract makes the interface explicit;{' '}
        <a href={getEnglishRoute('dataQualityChecks')}>quality checks</a> validate each produced
        batch against it; <a href={getEnglishRoute('pipelineFailureRecovery')}>failure recovery</a>{' '}
        handles the case where bad data still reached consumers.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="tradeoffs">
      <h2 id="tradeoffs">Edge cases and trade-offs</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Over-specification.</strong> A contract that freezes every byte makes producers
          afraid to change anything. Specify what consumers actually depend on, not the whole
          physical schema.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Consumers must declare dependencies.</strong> A contract test only protects the
          consumers that registered their expectations. Unknown readers are the ones that break
          quietly.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Versioning has a cost.</strong> Dual-writing and migrating consumers takes time;
          that cost is the price of not corrupting historical reports. Skip it only when no consumer
          reads the field.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Contracts are not a platform.</strong> For a handful of producers and consumers, a
          versioned file plus CI tests is enough. A registry becomes worth it when discovery and
          many-to-many dependencies appear.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Semantics need a reviewer.</strong> “Same schema, new meaning” is a human
          decision. Automate what can be checked, and require a named owner for what cannot.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="english-related" aria-labelledby="related">
      <h2 id="related">Related pages</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('dataQualityChecks')}>Data quality checks</a> — the runtime rules
          that decide whether a produced batch is fit to publish.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('pipelineFailureRecovery')}>Data pipeline failure recovery</a> —
          what to stop, replay and re-notify when broken data already reached consumers.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('lineageVsTaskDependency')}>Data lineage vs task dependency</a> —
          how to find every consumer that a contract change can reach.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('idempotentEtl')}>How to make an ETL job idempotent</a> — why
          re-running a producer after a contract change is safe only with an addressable write.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('pipelineObservability')}>Pipeline observability</a> — the
          long-running signals that show a contract change caused drift in production.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</EnglishArticleLayout>
